    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d259">

                <pb facs="0316" n="304" xml:id="pg304"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.00/3–245: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Salonika</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gwynn</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Salonika</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-03-02T18:00:00+02:00">March 2, 1945—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 3—10:25 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>26. The Regent<anchor n="81" xml:id="d259-f1"/> completed today a 3 days’ visit
                    to Salonika which appears to have been highly successful. His chief purpose was
                    to assert publicly and solemnly that Macedonia is an integral part of Greece and
                    must so remain. He also stressed Anglo-Greek ties. Enormous crowds evidenced
                    great enthusiasm and no untoward incident marred the occasion. Everyone seems
                    pleased and even the Communists find little to criticize.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gwynn</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d259-f1" n="81" xml:id="d259-f1-content">
                <p>Damaskinos, Archbishop of Athens.</p>
